Mandal Math

Ingredients: QANUN + MATH ROCK + BASS CLARINET + RIQ + CLEAN BARITONE GUITAR + TAPE ECHO

Creative Catalyst: The genre began during an acoustics residency in an unfinished civic arts center in Amman, where a qanun player rehearsed taksim-like modal phrases in a stairwell while a math-rock trio practiced irregular-meter guitar cells in a concrete rehearsal room below. The decisive experiment came when the guitarist stopped treating the qanun as a melodic layer and instead counted the lever changes themselves as structural events. Each mandal shift became a cue: extend the bar, remove a beat, change the guitar voicing, or redirect the bass clarinet. The resulting music did not alternate between “Middle Eastern” passages and “rock” passages. Its entire rhythmic grammar was built from the physical act of re-tuning notes mid-phrase.

Mandal Math is an asymmetrical chamber-rock form in which the qanun governs both pitch and architecture. The instrument’s plucked attacks, wide register, microtonal inflections, rapid tremolo, glissandi, and especially its small mandal levers determine how sections expand and contract. A lever change is not decorative ornamentation. It is a measurable compositional event that can trigger a meter change, a guitar rest, a bass-clarinet entrance, or a sudden harmonic reclassification.

The qanun is recorded close and dry enough to preserve plectrum attack, fingertip contact, bridge buzz, sympathetic ringing, and the tiny mechanical sound of mandals being moved. Wide stereo capture is avoided as the default; the instrument should feel physically concentrated rather than cinematic. A secondary room pair may be used only to reveal the geometry of the performance space during quieter sections.

Math rock supplies the temporal chassis: clean baritone guitar, clipped drum attacks, asymmetrical riffs, displaced accents, sudden rests, and phrase lengths that resolve through internal logic rather than four-bar symmetry. The guitar avoids saturated djent weight and oversized post-rock crescendos. Instead, it uses muted harmonics, open fifths, ringing dyads, tapped natural harmonics, brittle upper-register chords, and abrupt cutoffs that answer specific qanun notes.

Bass clarinet occupies the elastic low-middle register. It uses chalumeau fundamentals, breath noise, key percussion, multiphonics, narrow chromatic falls, and short pedal tones. It rarely doubles guitar roots. Its more important role is to connect the acoustic pitch field created by the qanun to the equal-tempered guitar without pretending the two systems are identical. When the qanun shifts a scale degree by a mandal, the clarinet may bend toward the new pitch instead of snapping instantly into tune.

Riq provides the principal percussive vocabulary. Dry skin strokes, edge taps, finger rolls, jingles, muted tek attacks, and brief open dum tones reinforce the internal grouping of each odd-meter phrase. A compact drum kit may appear, but it remains subordinate: kick, snare, closed hi-hat, and one floor tom only. Cymbal wash is avoided because it obscures the qanun’s upper transients and blurs the exact point where meter changes occur.

Tape echo is used selectively as a structural shadow. One qanun note, guitar harmonic, or clarinet breath may be sent into a short repeating delay whose feedback length matches the number of beats in the current phrase. When the meter changes, the delay is either cut abruptly or allowed to “miscount” across the new bar, making the transition physically audible.

The defining tension is calibrated instability. Every section feels mathematically intentional, but the tuning itself can shift underneath the count. The listener hears two kinds of movement at once: bars changing length and notes changing identity.

Genre DNA

Tempo & Pulse: 92–126 BPM, with frequent movement between 5/8, 7/8, 9/8, 10/8, 11/8, and asymmetrical mixed-meter phrases such as 3+2+2, 2+2+3, 4+3+2, or 3+3+2+3. Tempo normally remains fixed while bar length changes. Straight 4/4 may appear briefly as contrast but should not dominate.

Harmonic Palette: Hijaz, Rast, Bayati- and Nahawand-inspired modal colors approached as flexible pitch fields rather than fixed Western scales; open fifths, suspended seconds, neutral thirds, minor seconds, tritones, pedal tones, and deliberately unresolved dyads. Equal-tempered guitar notes may remain slightly “wrong” against the qanun when the friction is musically useful.

Production Signatures:

1. Mandal Trigger Map — selected qanun mandal changes are assigned arrangement consequences: add or remove a beat, mute guitar for one attack, open a clarinet multiphonic, or shorten the riq cycle.

2. False Downbeat Dyad — baritone guitar lands a clean two-note chord on an accent that sounds like beat one but is actually an interior point of the phrase; the qanun completes the real cycle afterward.

3. Clarinet Pitch Bridge — bass clarinet bends or shades between equal-tempered guitar pitch and qanun microtonal target, making tuning disagreement audible rather than hidden.

4. Counted Tape Spill — a short tape delay is calibrated to the outgoing meter. When the next phrase changes length, the old echoes continue across the new count until manually cut.

5. Jingle Blackout — riq jingles disappear during the densest qanun passages, leaving only dry skin attacks so upper-frequency information remains clear.

Dynamics: Intensity grows through denser qanun tremolo, faster alternation between mandal positions, shorter inter-phrase silences, more frequent meter changes, stronger guitar attack, and broader clarinet register. The music avoids predictable quiet-loud post-rock arcs. Peak energy may arrive through a tightly interlocked 11/8 section played at moderate volume.

Scene & Origin Lore

Mandal Math formed around a temporary arts program inside an unfinished municipal performing-arts complex in Amman. Construction had stalled after the main shell was completed, leaving bare concrete staircases, exposed conduit, numbered rehearsal doors, unfinished acoustic panels, steel handrails, chalk measurements, empty orchestra pits, stacked gypsum boards, and long corridors where sound carried between floors.

Qanun player Lina Haddad had been using a stair landing as a practice space because the close concrete reflections made every mandal movement and plectrum attack unusually distinct. Guitarist Omar Vale, drummer Hana Nasser, and bass-clarinetist Elias Trent were rehearsing an angular instrumental project two floors below. Their first jam collapsed almost immediately because the rock players kept assuming Lina’s phrases would resolve on familiar barlines.

The breakthrough came when Hana wrote the mandal changes down as if they were drum cues instead of pitch ornaments. One lever movement became “subtract one eighth-note.” Another meant “guitar mute.” A double shift on neighboring strings meant “hold the clarinet entrance until the next phrase.” This transformed the ensemble from musicians accompanying a qanun into a group whose entire form depended on watching the qanun player’s hands.

The band’s first public rehearsals were called calibration rooms. Audience members stood along taped measurement lines on the concrete floor while the group performed without a stage. Before each piece, Lina demonstrated the mandal positions that would control the structure. The point was not to explain the theory but to teach listeners which tiny physical gestures could suddenly reshape the entire rhythm.

The scene developed a visual language of pale concrete, brass qanun hardware, black cable, blue painter’s tape, red chalk marks, off-white work lights, dark plain clothing, steel handrails, handwritten meter diagrams, and instrument cases covered with construction dust. The unofficial rule is that every odd meter must be audible as phrasing, not displayed as virtuosity for its own sake.

