Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Lever Four Deletes a Beat

Story & Context: The opening piece begins in 9/8 grouped 4+3+2. Lina plays a repeating qanun figure whose fourth important pitch requires a visible mandal shift. Hana assigns that lever movement one structural consequence: every second time it occurs, the final eighth-note of the bar disappears. The ensemble therefore alternates between 9/8 and 8/8 without changing tempo or announcing the transition with a fill.

Omar uses a two-note baritone-guitar dyad that always lands where a conventional listener expects the downbeat, but Lina’s final two-note answer proves that the cycle has not actually restarted. Elias enters only after the first deleted beat with a bass-clarinet pitch that begins on the guitar’s equal-tempered note and bends downward toward Lina’s qanun degree.

In the middle section, the same mandal shift stops deleting a beat and instead silences the guitar for one attack. The listener hears the physical lever gesture remain constant while its formal meaning changes. The track ends with Lina moving the lever one final time and nobody reacting. The qanun note rings alone, detached from the rule the band had taught the listener.

Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Close documentary image of a qanun mandal lever labeled with a small handwritten “4,” blue tape on concrete marking eight and nine subdivisions, baritone-guitar headstock and bass-clarinet keys in shallow focus. No full lyrics; sparse spoken counting fragments limited to “four changes it,” “remove one,” “not the downbeat,” and “leave the last shift unanswered.”

Track 2: Eleven Tiles Across the Landing

Story & Context: A stair landing in the rehearsal building contains eleven rectangular floor tiles between two expansion joints. Hana turns the geometry into an 11/8 pulse grouped 3+3+2+3. The riq articulates only the first stroke of each group while Omar plays a clean baritone-guitar harmonic on every second grouping, creating a pattern that seems simpler than it is.

Lina uses a descending qanun line that changes one scale degree halfway across the eleven-count phrase by flipping two mandals in opposite directions. Elias responds with a multiphonic that contains both the old and new pitch regions at once. For several cycles the clarinet becomes the only instrument acknowledging both tunings simultaneously.

Halfway through, Hana removes all riq jingles and plays only skin taps. The meter remains identical but becomes harder to hear because its brightest markers vanish. Omar then reduces his line to one muted attack per eleven beats, forcing the qanun to carry both harmonic and rhythmic orientation.

The final section repeats the eleven-count phrase with no drum kit and no guitar. Qanun, bass clarinet, and hand-played riq maintain the form acoustically until the last expansion-joint accent.

Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Concrete stair landing with eleven rectangular floor tiles between two visible joints, qanun case against a steel rail, riq on one tile, bass clarinet shadow crossing the count. Sparse spoken fragments limited to “eleven across,” “three three two three,” “take away the jingles,” and “the floor still counts.”

Track 3: Echo Counts the Old Meter

Story & Context: The EP closer begins in 7/8 with one qanun note and one guitar harmonic sent into a tape echo calibrated to repeat across the seven-beat cycle. The band performs the first sixteen bars with almost no low register, allowing the delay pattern to become part of the perceived meter.

Without stopping the tape, Hana changes the live ensemble into 5/8. The old seven-based echoes continue spilling over the new phrase boundaries, producing a temporary rhythmic disagreement between recorded decay and present-time playing. Lina responds by narrowing the qanun register and reducing ornamentation while Elias uses breath-only clarinet attacks to mark the new five-beat shape.

Omar eventually stops playing entirely and manually changes the tape feedback so each repeat shortens. The old meter slowly loses authority instead of being cut off at once. In the final twenty bars the band enters a stable 10/8 phrase grouped 3+2+2+3, but no echo is used. The music has become rhythmically more complex while the production is at its driest.

The final event is not a chord. Lina performs one audible mandal shift after every instrument has stopped, followed by the tiny mechanical click of the lever settling into place.

